@node "A1200" "Retro Games THE A1200"
@{JCENTER}@{IMG "GUIDEDIR:files/32seonliner.png"}


You found a hidden gem ;)



@{IMG "GUIDEDIR:files/12.jpg"}

For the first time in many years we are getting a complete
new Amiga, sort of. It is emulation but it's really optimized
emulation! Latency is under 60ms for video and audio, 
typical RPI is 120ms and extreme low input latency,
this is achived with custom hardware, simply put the best.

Sysinfo around 260MIPS, Chris Smith says on a 68040
configuration you get about 60MHz depending on the load/application.

There is atleast one RTG game in the 25 game carousel.
There is also RESHOOT 3 ;)

I just wish they (Retro Games) would add a system where users
could add games/etc. to the carousel itself, it would be a
killer feature ! One can always dream...

This is a BIG deal, suddenly we have a cheap Amiga that
will sell in BIG numbers, everyone can get one and it's
complete (almost, no PSU). 

No hunting for most of us for a secondhand old A1200 that
requires even more money before it's usable and still it
be hard to get RTG/Chipset working seamless.

And it's budget friendly, this means many many more users!



@{IMG "GUIDEDIR:files/2.png"}

Front case and stickers, sorry for the quality, cut from
Retro Games latest video clip.



@{IMG "GUIDEDIR:files/3.png"}

Power LED green ;) and it's in action!


@{IMG "GUIDEDIR:files/4.png"}

I do not have a better picture of the board, but compared
to bellow THE A500 board you see it's not the same.
The new board has 4 USB on back and a internal.


@{IMG "GUIDEDIR:files/5.jpg"}@{IMG "GUIDEDIR:files/16.png"}



It even has the 3 LEDs board ;)

@{IMG "GUIDEDIR:files/7.png"}@{IMG "GUIDEDIR:files/8.png"}



It has pre-installed Worbench 3.1 with RTG 1280x720
This is a BIG deal, I think many more nice games will be
done thanks to having RTG out of the box.

So to use Workbench you need to plug in a USB stick, it
then copies it from internal flash to USB stick 
Workbench and Work (.HDF files), and runs from there and 
this allows you to customize it and you can have several 
USB sticks and switch Workbench setup ;) and even bring 
your own Workbench.

Since it's your own stick you can have the size you want
and not be limited! and also run the stick on a PC/Mac
with a emulator.

Workbench also have special application to control 
hardware like floppy disk, CD, CPU speed, Scaling, Eject
USB stick (running Workbench and you plug a USB stick it
appears on desktop).
PPaint is pre-installed.

I'm going todo a 32se setup when I get my hands on this
lovely machine, it be like in the 90s and my USB stick
is then going to be mounted on the internal USB port. 

I've been looking for info about the emulation, a video 
from gamescom by Scene World Magazine it looked like 8MB 
chip and 32MB fast is the maximum, but hunting some info 
it looks like you can even have your own AmiBerry config 
file on a USB stick and you can do more with the settings 
like 128/256MB fast RAM, I really hope this is true, 
information I found was in-official.

THE A500 board had 512MB RAM, A1200 probably has 1/2GB

@{IMG "GUIDEDIR:files/15.jpg"}
Workbench running in 1280x720

@{IMG "GUIDEDIR:files/9.png"}


@{IMG "GUIDEDIR:files/10.png"}
Retro Games video clip concludes with a truck fully loaded
with THE A1200 machines ;) holy moly ! ! !

Should I order one more !? ;)



@{FG shine}
*** ***
*********
**** ****
*******
*****
*
@{FG text}


@{JLEFT}
@endnode 




@node "3DFX" "Amiga MiSTer 3dfx"
@{JCENTER}@{IMG "GUIDEDIR:files/32seonliner.png"}

@{IMG "GUIDEDIR:files/amiga-corporation-2026mister3dfx.png"}

BigMig for MiSTer

A Big Box Amiga for the MiSTer board: the Minimig chipset in the
FPGA fabric, driven by a 68k that is not in the fabric at all.

BigMig replaces the in-fabric soft CPU with Emu68-A9, an ARMv7
just-in-time recompiler running bare-metal on the second ARM core
of the DE10-Nano's HPS. The Amiga custom chips - Agnus, Denise,
Paula, Gary, the CIAs - are the same cycle-accurate Minimig logic
they have always been. Only processor moved.

Having half the FPGA unused is not an option anymore. Now doing
the latest tests of BigMig 3Dx2.

3Dx2 is a dual pipeline GPU with 2 texture units implemented in 
the FPGA fabric that does the same calculations than a Voodoo 3
(announces as such) and comes with its own Warp3D and MiniGL libs.

Lu's Retro Extras (YouTube) has impressive demo of it running 
Quake and its really fluid and fast!



Maybe buy a second THE A1200 and put one in it or some clever
switch since there are alot of room left for a MiSTer.

However MiSTer is not cheap these days and is a board(s)<>box


Amigas FUTURE is really BRIGHT !!!!!



@{FG shine}
*** ***
*********
**** ****
*******
*****
*
@{FG text}


@{JLEFT}
@endnode 




@node "A500" "THE A500 mini"
@{JCENTER}@{IMG "GUIDEDIR:files/32seonliner.png"}

@{IMG "GUIDEDIR:files/a500minipj.jpg"}

I bought THE A500 mini when it was released and sold it within
weeks after the purchase, I'm not BIG fan of the carousel
system and been mini with no functional keys and no Workbench
and most letdown when you can't add anything to the carousel.

The last drop was when I tried to game with it, gamepad was
really bad and hard to configure own gamepad and in 2 player
mode it was such hassle(gamepad issues) I got rid of it.

I do see many looking for better cheap hardware for it ;)

Tang Nano 20K cheap FPGA board ! Looks like developent is
going forward, it even run AGA games.

Does all chipsets, max 2MB chip, 4MB fast RAM. You can
configure slowmem 1.5MB as well.

There is developent going on to maybe be able to have 5.5MB
fastmem if slowmem option is 0

Some Turbo options and video output mode PAL/NTSC

This is a very cheap FPGA solution about $40


Its crazy that this small board is more advanced than a stock
A1200 board...
 
See it running on MediaGamesRetroNerdstuff (YouTube)



@{FG shine}
*** ***
*********
**** ****
*******
*****
*
@{FG text}


@{JLEFT}
@endnode 




